{"title":"Northcairn Recycling","sub":"A battery recycling plant: is the market enough?","type":"Market entry","industry":"Industrial recycling","geo":"Sweden","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Utilisation · break-even · supply contracts","prompt":"Northcairn wants to build a lithium-ion battery recycling plant in northern Sweden that would recover lithium, nickel and cobalt from used batteries and factory scrap. The plant would cost $90M and process up to 20,000 tonnes a year. The board asks: should Northcairn build it?","clarify":[{"q":"Where does feedstock come from?","a":"Scrap from nearby battery factories today; end-of-life electric-car batteries later in the decade."},{"q":"Economics?","a":"After variable processing costs, each tonne earns about $1,800 from recovered metals and gate fees. Fixed costs are $21M a year."},{"q":"Contracts?","a":"Signed supply agreements cover 45% of capacity."},{"q":"Hurdle?","a":"Payback within eight years."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Northcairn build the plant?","branches":[{"head":"Feedstock","items":["Contracted vs uncontracted tonnes","Factory scrap now, car batteries later","Competing recyclers"]},{"head":"Economics per tonne","items":["Metal prices","Gate fees","Processing cost"]},{"head":"Plant","items":["Utilisation needed to break even","Payback at different utilisation"]}]},"hypothesis":"Recycling plants have high fixed costs, so profit depends on running near capacity. Generic market growth doesn't matter if Northcairn can't secure the batteries.","questions":[{"q":"Calculate annual operating profit at 50%, 75% and 90% utilisation, and the break-even utilisation.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"400.718pt\" height=\"163.890471pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 400.718 163.890471\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g